  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 1257 PM MDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nFriday...On Fri the upper pattern starts to shift slightly as the \nridge to the east flattens. This pattern shift will push the \nmoisture plume to the east, as an upper disturbance crosses \nColorado, tapping into the available moisture and provide for one \nmore day of shower and thunderstorm activity for the higher terrain \nand adjacent plains. High temps will climb into the 60s to around \n70F for the high valleys, and 70s to near 80F for the plains.\n\nSaturday and Sunday...The upper shortwave pushes east, and a ridge \nof high pressure builds in for both Sat and Sun. Models continue to \nshow very dry conditions for the area Sat into Sun, then isolated \nactivity returning to the Continental Divide Sunday night as another \nPacific trough moves onshore across the West Coast. Temps warm back \nup into the 70s for the high valleys, and upper 70s to mid 80s for \nthe plains.\n\nMonday and Tuesday: Upper trough crosses the Great Basin and into \nthe Rockies on Monday, then splits apart with the northern piece of \nenergy crossing the Rockies through Tuesday, while the southern \npiece closes off and drops down into the Desert SW. While this is \nall occurring, Hurricane Polo will track up the coastline of the CA \nBaja, feeding moisture up into the Desert SW. If this solution holds \ntrue, this will be another chance for some beneficial rainfall \nacross southeast and south central CO. As for temps, Mon will still \nbe a warm one, very similar to Sun, but by Tue high temps will drop \nas much as 10 to 15 degrees. Moore",
